1
00:00:06,919 --> 00:00:12,467
In 1606, a large trading ship struck
a rock off the coast of Lisbon, Portugal.

2
00:00:12,925 --> 00:00:16,179
As the vessel’s valuable contents
floated to the surface,

3
00:00:16,179 --> 00:00:19,182
locals flocked to the sea
to scavenge what they could.

4
00:00:19,474 --> 00:00:23,352
But it wasn’t precious metals, silks,
or gemstones they were after—

5
00:00:23,686 --> 00:00:25,605
it was black peppercorns.

6
00:00:26,314 --> 00:00:30,443
Black peppercorns are the small,
berry-like fruits of a tropical vine.

7
00:00:30,777 --> 00:00:33,613
The spice has a subtle
wood and citrus flavor,

8
00:00:33,613 --> 00:00:37,617
and a not-so-subtle spicy kick
that comes from piperine,

9
00:00:37,742 --> 00:00:41,996
a compound that binds to a heat
and pain receptor on our tongues.

10
00:00:42,622 --> 00:00:47,168
Humans have sought out this peppery
sensation for millennia.

11
00:00:48,002 --> 00:00:52,131
Thousands of years ago, people on the
Malabar Coast of what’s now India

12
00:00:52,131 --> 00:00:56,636
began growing Piper nigrum,
the black pepper that we eat today.

13
00:00:57,220 --> 00:01:01,099
In northeastern India,
growers planted Piper longum,

14
00:01:01,099 --> 00:01:03,851
a sweeter relative that’s now
far less common.

15
00:01:04,685 --> 00:01:09,690
Black, green, red, and white peppercorns
were all produced using the same plant

16
00:01:09,690 --> 00:01:11,692
by processing the fruit differently.

17
00:01:12,193 --> 00:01:14,779
To create black peppercorns, for example,

18
00:01:14,904 --> 00:01:17,240
growers picked the fruits
before they ripened

19
00:01:17,240 --> 00:01:19,867
and left them to dry and darken
in the sun.

20
00:01:20,576 --> 00:01:22,787
In India, pepper was prized,

21
00:01:22,787 --> 00:01:26,249
both as a culinary spice
and as a medicinal plant.

22
00:01:26,374 --> 00:01:30,837
Ayurvedic texts from 8 millennia ago
praised its healing properties,

23
00:01:30,837 --> 00:01:32,839
especially for digestive issues.

24
00:01:33,297 --> 00:01:38,511
Early migrants and traders helped move
pepper from India to Indonesia and beyond.

25
00:01:38,719 --> 00:01:40,763
Egyptians were early adopters—

26
00:01:40,763 --> 00:01:44,976
the spice was found in the nostrils
of the 3,000-year-old mummy

27
00:01:44,976 --> 00:01:47,645
of Pharaoh Ramesses II.

28
00:01:47,937 --> 00:01:51,774
Black pepper reached China and Rome
over 2 millennia ago

29
00:01:51,816 --> 00:01:55,027
via the Silk Road
and was quickly embraced.

30
00:01:55,278 --> 00:01:59,115
A Roman cookbook from the
4th or 5th century BCE

31
00:01:59,198 --> 00:02:03,452
called for pepper in 81%
of the 400 recipes.

32
00:02:03,828 --> 00:02:07,039
And when Visigoths captured Rome
in 410 AD,

33
00:02:07,415 --> 00:02:12,461
they demanded over 3,000 pounds of black
peppercorns as part of the city’s ransom.

34
00:02:13,171 --> 00:02:16,674
In the Middle Ages,
Alexandria, Venice, and Genoa

35
00:02:16,757 --> 00:02:20,219
grew rich on the pepper
pouring in and out of their ports.

36
00:02:20,720 --> 00:02:24,682
The wealthy piled pepper,
along with other spices, onto their food.

37
00:02:24,932 --> 00:02:28,561
Myths spread that the pepper
grew on trees guarded by serpents—

38
00:02:28,561 --> 00:02:30,730
a justification for the high price.

39
00:02:31,564 --> 00:02:33,065
In the late 15th century,

40
00:02:33,065 --> 00:02:36,152
Lisbon became Europe's new leader
in the pepper trade

41
00:02:36,152 --> 00:02:41,407
after Vasco da Gama discovered a direct
sea route connecting Portugal to India.

42
00:02:41,866 --> 00:02:45,286
But this pepper dominance
wasn't without contest.

43
00:02:45,703 --> 00:02:50,166
Over the following two centuries,
Spain, England, Portugal, and Holland

44
00:02:50,291 --> 00:02:55,421
battled to gain control over spices
and the people who grew them.

45
00:02:55,755 --> 00:02:57,965
Black pepper was also among the spices

46
00:02:57,965 --> 00:03:01,636
that sent Christopher Columbus
searching for a new route to India.

47
00:03:01,761 --> 00:03:06,057
Upon landing in the Americas,
he thought he’d found the spice—

48
00:03:06,182 --> 00:03:09,185
though he was actually biting
into a much hotter, larger,

49
00:03:09,185 --> 00:03:12,647
and completely unrelated fruit: the chili.

50
00:03:12,980 --> 00:03:16,859
Experts believe that ancient civilizations
in what is today Mexico

51
00:03:16,984 --> 00:03:20,071
first domesticated chilies
many millennia earlier.

52
00:03:20,488 --> 00:03:24,325
Like black pepper, chilies
activate heat and pain receptors,

53
00:03:24,325 --> 00:03:28,162
but with a more potent
chemical compound called capsaicin,

54
00:03:28,537 --> 00:03:32,667
leading Europeans to dub
this hot fruit “pepper” as well.

55
00:03:33,251 --> 00:03:37,338
Unlike black pepper, chilies
happily grew in a variety of climates,

56
00:03:37,463 --> 00:03:40,049
which soon made them
the far cheaper option.

57
00:03:40,299 --> 00:03:44,804
In India, chilies were embraced, becoming
a standard ingredient and garnish.

58
00:03:44,971 --> 00:03:48,891
And in China, chili peppers became
synonymous with Sichuan cuisine,

59
00:03:49,016 --> 00:03:50,893
alongside Sichuan peppers,

60
00:03:50,893 --> 00:03:54,355
another unrelated spicy plant
labeled “pepper.”

61
00:03:54,730 --> 00:03:56,357
By the mid-17th century,

62
00:03:56,357 --> 00:03:59,402
black pepper production
had spread well beyond India

63
00:03:59,568 --> 00:04:01,404
and the cost of pepper fell.

64
00:04:01,862 --> 00:04:06,284
But while it lost its allure as a status
symbol, it remained in wide use.

65
00:04:06,534 --> 00:04:10,913
In France, a new culinary movement
favored recipes lacking spices,

66
00:04:10,955 --> 00:04:14,250
with the exception of two:
salt and pepper.

67
00:04:14,583 --> 00:04:18,587
The trend spread, bringing
the now seeming inseparable pair

68
00:04:18,587 --> 00:04:21,424
to tabletops across Europe
and North America.

69
00:04:22,341 --> 00:04:26,304
Today, black pepper makes up 20%
of the global spice trade.

70
00:04:26,387 --> 00:04:28,848
It stands among many other heat options.

71
00:04:29,098 --> 00:04:33,686
We can spice up our meals with chilies
in salsas, harissa, and gochujang.

72
00:04:33,936 --> 00:04:36,689
Or we could simply reach
for the pepper shaker.

73
00:04:37,440 --> 00:04:41,402
So, how did salt come to join pepper
at so many tables?

